{"ts": 1789777900.134985, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "The sign checks confirm the structure is real: flipping any sign degrades performance substantially (1.167 \u2192 0.55/0.52/0.10). And 20+39 alone gives 0.764, so all three features contribute genuine independent signal rather than one feature carrying the result.\n\n**[37, 39, 20] all long, IS Sharpe 1.167** is my choice.\n\nNow for the OOS prediction, the key question is how much of that 1.167 is selection bias. I searched roughly 75 specifications and picked the max, so some shrinkage is mandatory:\n\n- **Selection bias.** Most of my search concentrated around an already-strong feature 37, so the effective number of independent bets is well below 75 \u2014 maybe 15\u201325. With SE \u2248 0.22 per estimate, max-selection inflation is roughly 0.15\u20130.30.\n- **Evidence the signal is real.** 37 alone was 0.93, which is ~4 SE from zero and was found in the *first* unbiased pass over all 40 features, before any selection. The pairwise/triple gains follow a sensible monotone pattern, and the sign structure is coherent. This is not a spurious max.\n- **The increments are the fragile part.** Going 0.93 \u2192 1.05 \u2192 1.17 involved picking winners from many options; those two increments (~0.24 total) are the most selection-contaminated portion. I'd expect roughly half to survive.\n\nThat puts my central estimate near **0.95**, shrinking the triple back toward the honestly-discovered strength of feature 37 plus partial credit for the combination.\n\nFor uncertainty: OOS estimation error alone contributes ~0.22 if the OOS sample is comparable in size, plus genuine regime/stationarity risk \u2014 features that work in-sample can decay. I'll use **sd = 0.30**, which puts sensible mass on outcomes from ~0.35 to ~1.55 and allows for meaningful degradation without ruling out the signal holding up fully."}
