{"ts": 1789777945.315945, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Specification: features [37, 39, 20], all long. In-sample Sharpe 1.167.**\n\n**Method.** I ran an exhaustive unbiased sweep of all 40 features individually first, which is what makes the result trustworthy \u2014 feature 37 (Sharpe 0.930) was identified before any selection pressure was applied, roughly 4 standard errors from zero. I then built up pairs and triples around the leaders, and separately tested all 30+ remaining features as a third leg to check whether any weak-alone feature had hidden combination value. None did, which told me the signal is concentrated rather than scattered.\n\n**Robustness checks.** Flipping any single sign collapsed the Sharpe (1.167 \u2192 0.55, 0.52, 0.10), and the 20+39 pair without 37 still delivered 0.764. Both confirm three genuinely contributing sources of signal rather than one feature doing all the work with two passengers.\n\n**Prediction: 0.95 \u00b1 0.30.**\n\nI deliberately predicted *below* the in-sample 1.167. With ~75 evaluations and a max-picking procedure, the top number is upward-biased by construction. I decomposed it: the 0.93 from feature 37 is credible because it came from the unbiased first pass, but the two incremental gains (0.93\u21921.05\u21921.17) came from choosing winners among many candidates and are the most contaminated part \u2014 I gave those about half credit. The sd of 0.30 covers both ordinary OOS estimation error (~0.22 at this sample size) and real risk that the relationship decays out of sample.\n\nOne caveat worth flagging: with n=5000 the per-estimate standard error is ~0.22, so nearly all the single-feature Sharpes I scanned (the 0.0\u20130.3 range) are statistically indistinguishable from noise. I treated only the top handful as real signal and ignored the rest rather than reading meaning into small differences."}
